# SKILL.md — Example Skill Template

This is a template for creating a new skill. Skills live in the `skills/` directory
and define custom capabilities for your agent.

## Skill Structure

Create a new directory under `skills/` with your skill name:

```
skills/
└── my-skill/
    ├── SKILL.md        # This file - skill documentation
    ├── SKILL.toml      # Optional - structured skill manifest
    └── scripts/        # Optional - supporting scripts
        └── tool.sh
```

## SKILL.md Format

```markdown
# My Skill

**Description:** What this skill does
**Version:** 1.0.0
**Author:** Your Name
**Tags:** automation, deployment

## Tools

### deploy
**Kind:** shell
**Description:** Deploy the application
**Command:** ./scripts/deploy.sh

### check-status
**Kind:** http
**Description:** Check service health
**Command:** https://api.example.com/health

## Prompts

Use these prompts when:
- The user asks about deployment
- The user mentions production issues
```

## SKILL.toml Format (Alternative)

```toml
[skill]
name = "my-skill"
description = "What this skill does"
version = "1.0.0"
author = "Your Name"
tags = ["automation", "deployment"]

[[tools]]
name = "deploy"
description = "Deploy the application"
kind = "shell"
command = "./scripts/deploy.sh"

[[tools]]
name = "check-status"
description = "Check service health"
kind = "http"
command = "https://api.example.com/health"

[[prompts]]
text = """Use these prompts when:
- The user asks about deployment
- The user mentions production issues
"""
```

## Tool Kinds

- **shell** — Execute a shell command
- **http** — Make an HTTP request
- **script** — Run a script file

## Best Practices

1. Keep skills focused on one domain
2. Document all tools clearly
3. Include example usage in prompts
4. Test scripts before committing
5. Use relative paths from the skill directory

## Example: DevOps Skill

```markdown
# DevOps Automation

**Description:** Common DevOps tasks and deployments
**Version:** 1.0.0
**Tags:** devops, deployment, docker

## Tools

### docker-build
**Kind:** shell
**Description:** Build Docker image
**Command:** docker build -t {{image}} .
Args:
  - image: Image name and tag

### k8s-apply
**Kind:** shell
**Description:** Apply Kubernetes manifests
**Command:** kubectl apply -f k8s/

## Prompts

When the user asks about deployment:
1. Check current git status
2. Run tests if available
3. Build the Docker image
4. Apply Kubernetes manifests
5. Verify deployment status
```
